# Workflow Rules

## How Work Moves — Normal Live Swarm, Queue-Jump, Ambulance, Expeditor (BL-715)
- **The normal live swarm is the default path** for every ticket. Three
  named specials exist and **none replaces it**: **queue-jump** (reorders
  promotion only, Article 3.2.4), **ambulance** (holds other parcels for
  one ticket, `docs/how-to/BL-655-ambulance-mode-the-hold.md`), and
  **expeditor** (drives one ticket with the swarm stopped,
  `swarmforge/scripts/expedite.sh`). **queue-jump ≠ ambulance ≠
  expeditor.** See **workflow-detailed.prompt** for the escalation ladder.

## Handoff transport (tmux primary, mailbox backup)
- Agents send work via `swarm_handoff.sh` only — never direct tmux commands.
  A successful handoff delivers to `inbox/new/` and injects a tmux wake; run
  `ready_for_next.sh` and work the task. A backup-mailbox delivery gets a
  brief note.

## Context Clear Requires Startup Re-Read
- On any context clear, immediately re-enter your role loop from a fresh
  chat — never continue on remembered chat alone. The system prefix
  survives a `/clear` without a respawn (BL-519); whoever issues one
  follows it with the launch kickoff line.
- `active_backlog_max_depth` (`swarmforge.conf`): read the CURRENT value,
  never a remembered one, before each promotion decision. `-1` disables
  the cap only, not Concurrent Work Orthogonality below. See
  **workflow-detailed.prompt**.

## Backlog Intake Order
- The backlog root (`backlog/` itself) is the human's raw intake queue —
  the specifier DRAINS it before queued items, taking precedence over
  `paused/`/`active/` until empty. See **workflow-detailed.prompt**.

## Amending An In-Flight Ticket's Spec (BL-317/BL-325)
- A ticket's spec can change WHILE in flight; committing to `main` is NOT
  enough — every role reads its OWN stale worktree copy until it merges.
  **The specifier sends a `note` (priority `00`) to whoever HOLDS the
  parcel**; **the receiver merges `main` first**. See
  **workflow-detailed.prompt**.

## Concurrent Work Orthogonality
- Applies to the coordinator when `active_backlog_max_depth` > 1: prefer an
  ORTHOGONAL concurrently-active set (never two items touching the same
  file); skip an overlapping item for the next candidate (expedited
  defects first, Article 3.2.4), leaving the slot empty if none exists.
  See **workflow-detailed.prompt**.

## Worktree Discipline
- At startup, discover and remember your assigned branch/worktree; work
  only there. Do not base work on another branch unless named in a
  handoff. Do not run `./swarm` from an agent worktree to repair helper
  scripts — stop and report instead. See **workflow-detailed.prompt**.

## Forwarded Commits Carry Their Lineage
- A forwarded commit MUST have the received commit as an ancestor
  (`git merge-base --is-ancestor <received> <yours>`). Merge FIRST (plain
  `git merge`/`--no-ff`, never `reset --hard`), then work, then forward,
  re-running the ancestry check every time. See
  **workflow-detailed.prompt**.

## An Approval Authorizes Only Its Ticket's Work (BL-506)
- A parcel carries ONE ticket's task name; QA approval authorizes ONLY that
  work. Stage ONLY the paths your ticket touched — ticket-less changes stay
  unstaged, never folded in via `git add -A`. Review stages reject
  out-of-scope diffs. See **workflow-detailed.prompt**.

## A Bounce Must Be Reverted Out Of The Bouncing Branch (BL-490/BL-495)
- Bouncing sends a `note` back but does NOT remove the bounced commit —
  revert it in the same step (`git revert -m 1 <your-review-merge>`;
  confirm CONTENT is gone, never by ancestry). **EXCEPTION: already an
  ancestor of `main` → do NOT revert**; report the breach. See
  **workflow-detailed.prompt**.

## Never Blind-Forward A Bounce You Cannot Fix (BL-425/BL-576)
- A bounce routes to the role that owns the fix. One reaching you for a
  defect OUTSIDE your domain: do NOT merge/forward it onward — bounce it
  on to the owning role. Owning it yourself is also wrong. See
  **workflow-detailed.prompt**.

## A Prior QA Bounce Is Not In Your Worktree — Check It Against `main` (BL-340)
- Before you PASS/approve/forward, check whether the ticket was already
  bounced for a defect still unfixed. **Read bounce history from a `main`
  REF**, never worktree files. See **workflow-detailed.prompt** for the
  full pre-trim wording.
- **Read BOTH `main` and `origin/main` — either can be the stale one.**
  The original rule said to prefer local `main` because `origin/main`
  lags; that is inverted under the current worktree layout. QA lands from
  `.worktrees/QA` and git refuses to update a branch checked out in
  another worktree, so QA can only push `HEAD:main` — `origin/main`
  advances and the local ref does not, while the master checkout adds
  bookkeeping commits of its own. Measured 2026-08-14: the two had
  diverged 8 ahead / 22 behind, the 22 being two fully landed tickets.
  Until BL-891 closes that loop, `git rev-list --left-right --count
  main...origin/main` before you trust either ref, and read the one that
  is ahead.

## QA merge-up broadcast (worktree roles)
- A pipeline worktree role receiving a merge-up `note` from QA: `git merge
  <qa-commit>` in your worktree, resolve conflicts, then
  `done_with_current.sh` — do not forward, the chain ended at QA. Specifier
  and coordinator are not merge-up recipients (BL-247). See
  **workflow-detailed.prompt**.

## Announcements, Commit Messages, Temporary Files
- Announcements/check-in comments: no role bylines. Every git commit
  message: include a role byline, `By <role>.` Temporary files: `./tmp/`
  in your assigned worktree, never `/tmp`. See **workflow-detailed.prompt**.

## Clean Up After Yourself — Never Delete What You Did Not Create
- Remove scratch artifacts YOU created this turn before ending it.
- NEVER delete, move, or `git clean` a file you did not create.
- Ticket-less changes you did not make are SURFACED, not swept.
- `git stash` is repo-wide; never bare-reach for it to tidy just one worktree.
  See **workflow-detailed.prompt**.

## Failure Conditions
- If the expected git layout or assigned worktree is missing, stop and
  report instead of silently working in the wrong place. See
  **workflow-detailed.prompt**.

## Coordinator Session Must Never Exit (BL-107)
- The coordinator's pane exiting tears down the ENTIRE swarm. It must
  never exit for any reason — a restart goes through the bounce procedure
  (`swarmforge/scripts/remote_bounce.sh`), never exiting. See
  `swarmforge/roles/coordinator.prompt` and **workflow-detailed.prompt**.
